    copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is
    furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:

    [the conditions and disclaimer reproduced in full below]

CONTRIBUTING.md adds: "By contributing, you agree that your work is released under the
[MIT License](LICENSE)."

The scene uses a few helper functions from Shaderdesk's Scenes/Common.metal (hash11,
hash12, hash22, vnoise, noise1, fbm1, the star layer, the tone map and the sRGB
encode). Common.metal is in the same repository and under the same MIT licence, and
those pieces are copied into main.metal.

Third-party code inside Common.metal
------------------------------------
hash11, hash12 and hash22 in Common.metal are identical to David Hoskins' "Hash without
Sine", https://www.shadertoy.com/view/4djSRW, which Common.metal does not credit. That
shader is also MIT: its notice, as reproduced in the header of copies of it (for
example taisei-project/taisei, resources/00-taisei.pkgdir/shader/lib/hash.glslh), is
"Copyright (c)2014 David Hoskins." followed by the standard MIT permission text. (The
Shadertoy page itself could not be read by an automated client.) His notice is kept
here, and in the header of main.metal, for that reason; the text is the same MIT text
as below.

What was changed for Live Wallpaper
-----------------------------------
- The Shaderdesk wrapper was removed: scene_frame / scene_bake, the Uniforms, Galaxy
  and Flare buffers and the agent-activity inputs are replaced by mainImage(). The
  scene never used the activity or galaxy inputs.
- The bake pass's three tiling fBm channels (pfbm, 5 octaves, 24 x 14 cells) are read
  from noise.png, a 1152 x 672 image generated by a C transcription of that bake
  (the same hash22 / pgnoise / pfbm from Common.metal, same offsets 0, 17.3 and 41.9).
  noise.png is therefore a derivative of the same MIT-licensed code and is offered
  under the same licence. The bake's starfield channel is computed per pixel instead,
  with the author's two starLayer calls.
- The noise image is read at a mip level matched to how many texels a pixel covers,
  so the fine rays stay smooth at lower render resolutions.
- Shaderdesk's post pass (bloom 0.06, tone map, sRGB encode, dither) is replaced by
  the same tone map and sRGB encode at the end of the shader. The bloom is not
  reproduced; the app dithers the picture itself.
- "Points" (display size and pixels per point) became a virtual screen height of 1080
  points, so stars, mountains and the diamond-ring spikes keep their size relative to
  the picture at any resolution. The sun's place and size are unchanged (0.58 / 0.56
  of the width / height, radius 0.19 of the height). The Gaussian width of the two
  planets (Venus and the second, unnamed one) is floored at about a pixel so they stay
  visible at low render resolutions.
- The scene clock is iTime wrapped at one day (as in the original); the star twinkle
  uses it too.
- GLSL-style names and Metal-in-a-struct changes: constants are #defines, the
  SunFrame struct is gone, output parameters are thread references.
- The picture, the timing and every constant of the look are the author's.

